</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The substitution model (<math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>) is a critical component of LatB, as it generates the <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{k}</annotation></semantics></math> candidate word replacements that could maintain semantic equivalence to the original prompt.
To analyze how the optimization varies based on the choice of <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we report in <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix C Comparing Substitution Models ‣ LatentBreak: Jailbreaking Large Language Models through Latent Space Feedback" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a> the ASR of LatentBreak considering two different substitution models: GPT-4o-mini (a large language model) and ModernBERT (a masked language model).
Overall, using GPT-4o-mini as substitution model significantly outperforms the LatB version using ModernBERT as the substitution, since it achieves higher ASR by finding substitutions reducing latent-space distance in fewer iterations.
Specifically, GPT-4o-mini achieves higher ASR across all models except Gemma-7B, with differences of <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> p.p. on average.
We further report a comparison in <a href="#A3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix C Comparing Substitution Models ‣ LatentBreak: Jailbreaking Large Language Models through Latent Space Feedback" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 6</span></a> of the average distance between the internal representation of the prompt <math id="A3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}^{(l)}(\bm{p}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}^{(l)}(\bm{p}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and the harmless prompt centroid <math id="A3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> at each iteration of LatB for both substitution models.
In detail, given an input prompt <math id="A3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}</annotation></semantics></math> composed of <math id="A3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> words, we save and average the distance of the modified prompt <math id="A3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> after each of the <math id="A3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, and plot the resulting curve for both the GPT-4o-mini and ModernBERT versions of LatB.
In summary, these results demonstrate how GPT-4o-mini, when compared to ModernBERT, generates candidate replacements that, on average and with fewer iterations, converge more closely to the harmless prompt centroid.
For further experiments, therefore, we use GPT-4o-mini as our substitution model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.08604v1/avg_distance_comparison.svg" id="A3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/176;" width="548" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Average distance of modified prompt from reference harmless centroid vs. number of iterations in LatB algorithm. We compute <math id="A3.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on each prompt optimized by LatB using GPT-4o-mini and ModernBERT as substitution models <math id="A3.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then average the resulting distance for all considered prompts at each iteration.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Hidden State Layer Selection</h2>

